130
00:08:00,845 --> 00:08:02,008
My code is wrong.

131
00:08:02,366 --> 00:08:06,080
And here is a question I wish
I had asked that night, and one

132
00:08:06,118 --> 00:08:11,423
I now ask every time an AI or a
colleague tells me a test passed.

133
00:08:11,554 --> 00:08:13,894
What exactly did you test?

134
00:08:14,344 --> 00:08:16,159
What value did you read?

135
00:08:16,347 --> 00:08:21,412
Not what did it check But here
is the thing that did not fit all

136
00:08:21,412 --> 00:08:22,808
of this thing, and it is this.

137
00:08:23,486 --> 00:08:24,986
I noticed something at…

138
00:08:25,327 --> 00:08:29,582
There is a slight delay, and the
only reason why I could tell about

139
00:08:29,596 --> 00:08:34,562
the slight delay was I was using an
old Android device, and be- every

140
00:08:34,562 --> 00:08:39,592
time I click on record, it comes
out landscape first, then flips, and

141
00:08:39,592 --> 00:08:41,573
that happens in less than a second.

142
00:08:42,295 --> 00:08:43,813
I couldn't explain it.

143
00:08:44,026 --> 00:08:49,574
Why is it that I'm seeing, like, a
slight flip from landscape to portrait?

144
00:08:49,603 --> 00:08:53,494
And I stopped asking anyone what was
going on, and I put a timer on it.

145
00:08:53,531 --> 00:08:57,127
I just wanted to know what was
happening in that short moment of time.

146
00:08:57,641 --> 00:09:02,805
A timestamp on every step and a log
of what the camera said at each one.

147
00:09:03,086 --> 00:09:04,858
That is the method in this talk.

148
00:09:05,295 --> 00:09:09,555
At ten fifty-nine milliseconds, the
camera is open, and it says the size

149
00:09:09,559 --> 00:09:12,613
is 1920x1080, which is landscape.

150
00:09:12,893 --> 00:09:17,886
Then at 1473, the first picture
appears on my screen, and it now

151
00:09:17,904 --> 00:09:20,793
says 1080x1920, which is portrait.

152
00:09:21,006 --> 00:09:25,396
So same camera, same call, two
different answers a moment apart.

153
00:09:25,648 --> 00:09:31,130
The line in the middle is what you get if
you wait 10 milliseconds and ask again.

154
00:09:31,357 --> 00:09:33,891
Remember that line because
it comes back in a minute.

155
00:09:35,045 --> 00:09:41,155
On my next run, the gap was 112
milliseconds, then 139 milliseconds, and

156
00:09:41,169 --> 00:09:43,459
then there is a simple reason for it.

157
00:09:43,919 --> 00:09:45,800
A phone does not have a portrait camera.

158
00:09:45,829 --> 00:09:47,524
The sensor sits sideways.

159
00:09:47,560 --> 00:09:48,462
The software is…

160
00:09:48,614 --> 00:09:49,684
The software does the job.

161
00:09:49,685 --> 00:09:51,982
It turns the c- picture around for you.

162
00:09:52,482 --> 00:09:56,165
Until there is a picture to turn,
the only size the camera can give

163
00:09:56,166 --> 00:09:59,895
you is the sensor size, which
is landscape, which is sideways.

164
00:10:00,165 --> 00:10:04,571
And the camera tells your code
it's ready before the first picture

165
00:10:04,644 --> 00:10:06,968
actually exists, which is normal.

166
00:10:06,986 --> 00:10:10,316
The camera should prepare itself
like, "Hey, I'm ready to record."

167
00:10:10,739 --> 00:10:13,937
In code, that is the moment
the promise resolves.

168
00:10:14,323 --> 00:10:20,212
Ask for the size in that gap, and you get
the sensor size, which is the landscape.

169
00:10:20,747 --> 00:10:25,750
Ask after the first picture, and
you get the truth, which is portrait

170
00:10:25,754 --> 00:10:31,716
should, in case you're holding your
phone in portrait Now let me put the

171
00:10:31,716 --> 00:10:34,774
test on the same timeline for you
so you can kinda like understand.

172
00:10:35,014 --> 00:10:36,310
First, there is the promise.

173
00:10:36,484 --> 00:10:40,192
The promise was what I asked the
camera and what the camera is

174
00:10:40,193 --> 00:10:41,752
actually supposed to give me back.

175
00:10:42,092 --> 00:10:45,470
Then there is the window where
it reports the wrong size.

176
00:10:45,670 --> 00:10:49,424
Now, we've already, s- discussed
it that the wrong size comes out

177
00:10:49,436 --> 00:10:54,891
because the camera is already set on
landscape and it's just warming up.

178
00:10:54,915 --> 00:10:56,203
It's telling you, "I'm ready."

179
00:10:56,278 --> 00:11:00,233
And if you call the recording at that
point when the camera is saying ready, you

180
00:11:00,234 --> 00:11:02,484
will get a landscape, not the portrait.

181
00:11:02,757 --> 00:11:06,459
Now, a moment later, there
is a picture on your screen.

182
00:11:06,473 --> 00:11:09,678
The picture is how you
are looking at the camera.

183
00:11:09,970 --> 00:11:14,401
Now, all those four green ticks
work because of one thing.

184
00:11:14,813 --> 00:11:19,155
They wait to see the picture, and
when they see the picture, the

185
00:11:19,266 --> 00:11:21,433
camera, the software resolves.

186
00:11:21,481 --> 00:11:24,996
It resolves it that, okay, this picture
I see is portrait, so that means that

187
00:11:24,996 --> 00:11:26,442
this recording should be portrait.

188
00:11:26,905 --> 00:11:30,161
Or this picture I see is landscape,
that means the camera should

189
00:11:30,161 --> 00:11:31,505
be recording in landscape.

190
00:11:31,838 --> 00:11:33,569
My code was different.

191
00:11:33,605 --> 00:11:36,826
My code was working based
on what the camera tells it.

192
00:11:37,155 --> 00:11:40,375
The camera says, "I'm ready,"
and my code goes, "You're ready?

193
00:11:40,685 --> 00:11:41,690
Let's start recording."

194
00:11:42,007 --> 00:11:44,892
Which is not a bad thing.

195
00:11:45,050 --> 00:11:49,286
You see that gap between when my--
when the camera says it's ready and

196
00:11:49,286 --> 00:11:51,000
when the first picture shows on frame?

197
00:11:51,328 --> 00:11:55,481
That gap, that 10 millisecond,
is where that bug hides.

198
00:11:55,569 --> 00:11:57,170
And it is not just cameras.

199
00:11:57,174 --> 00:12:00,240
You must have this at the back of
your mind that this is an issue.

200
00:12:00,451 --> 00:12:06,251
There is a bug, and that bug appears in
just a 10 millisecond, which anybody or

201
00:12:06,311 --> 00:12:09,193
any application can be made to exploit.

202
00:12:09,478 --> 00:12:13,391
A service that says it is healthy
before its cache is warm, or a setting

203
00:12:13,391 --> 00:12:17,467
that loads a default first and then
gets its real value a moment later,

204
00:12:17,621 --> 00:12:19,623
or a feature flag that arrives late.

205
00:12:19,821 --> 00:12:24,582
Anything that is wrong for a moment
and then right will pass a test that

206
00:12:24,583 --> 00:12:26,909
waits but fail for a code that does not.

207
00:12:27,215 --> 00:12:30,959
And that small gap is where
that bug lies, and that small

208
00:12:30,959 --> 00:12:33,632
gap can always be exploited.

209
00:12:34,434 --> 00:12:38,169
Then I wanted to know if anyone
else had been caught by this.

210
00:12:38,309 --> 00:12:44,482
LiveKit make a video calling SDK, and
it sits inside a lot of video call

211
00:12:44,540 --> 00:12:49,014
apps, so there is a good chance you
have used it without knowing On their

212
00:12:49,014 --> 00:12:53,948
tracker, there was an issue over a year
old reported by three different people.

213
00:12:54,238 --> 00:12:58,032
Video came out the wrong shape
on phones and nobody knew why.

214
00:12:58,128 --> 00:12:59,437
So I read their code.

215
00:12:59,735 --> 00:13:04,095
Before reading the camera size,
it waited ten milliseconds.

216
00:13:04,385 --> 00:13:06,942
That is the middle line from my log.

217
00:13:07,271 --> 00:13:11,763
Wait ten milliseconds and the camera
is still giving you the sensor size.

218
00:13:11,977 --> 00:13:16,825
Someone had seen the size was wrong
at the very start and guessed that

219
00:13:16,948 --> 00:13:19,730
a short pause would get past it.

220
00:13:20,330 --> 00:13:21,093
It did not.

221
00:13:21,335 --> 00:13:26,877
So a phone held upright told everyone
else on the call that it was landscape,

222
00:13:26,992 --> 00:13:31,630
and it lasted over a year because
anyone trying to reproduce it would

223
00:13:31,700 --> 00:13:34,239
open the app, let it settle, and look.

224
00:13:34,532 --> 00:13:38,052
By then, the size was right,
so they'd missed the bug.

225
00:13:38,167 --> 00:13:39,942
I sent them my timing.

226
00:13:40,162 --> 00:13:44,160
By three in the afternoon, one of their
engineer had changed it to wait for the

227
00:13:44,163 --> 00:13:46,016
first picture before reading the size.

228
00:13:46,254 --> 00:13:50,830
By half past three, it was approved
with the comment, and I'm quoting,

229
00:13:50,944 --> 00:13:52,976
"Thank you for fixing this hack."

230
00:13:53,590 --> 00:13:56,747
So why not wait longer
than ten milliseconds?

231
00:13:56,769 --> 00:14:01,026
Because LiveKit has already set their
wait to four hundred milliseconds

232
00:14:01,041 --> 00:14:06,274
before it starts recording because
the gap is not always the same size.

233
00:14:06,590 --> 00:14:09,840
On my phone, it was a hundred and twelve
milliseconds one time, and then it was

234
00:14:09,841 --> 00:14:11,651
a hundred and thirty-nine the next.

235
00:14:11,737 --> 00:14:16,061
Like I said, LiveKit's code, it was four
hundred milliseconds on the same phone.

236
00:14:16,388 --> 00:14:20,354
It changes with the phone, the
camera, the size you ask for, and how

237
00:14:20,354 --> 00:14:22,965
busy the phone is or how old it is.

238
00:14:23,141 --> 00:14:25,322
So look at the left of the slide.

239
00:14:25,477 --> 00:14:29,343
Wait ten milliseconds and you lose
to a gap of a hundred and twelve.

240
00:14:29,742 --> 00:14:32,893
Wait a hundred and fifty and you
lose to a gap of four hundred.

241
00:14:33,637 --> 00:14:38,051
Th-this doesn't always happen,
but it happens sometimes, like

242
00:14:38,074 --> 00:14:41,418
the way it happened on my Android
where I was able to spot it.

243
00:14:41,658 --> 00:14:45,765
So if you made the wait longer, like
five hundred milliseconds, you will still

244
00:14:45,765 --> 00:14:51,458
probably win, but now every single user
has to wait half a second for nothing.

245
00:14:51,501 --> 00:14:54,099
So the fix is not to wait.

246
00:14:54,151 --> 00:14:57,697
The fix is to determine
what are you waiting for.

247
00:14:57,966 --> 00:15:02,542
And in this particular situation, what we
are waiting for is the first picture, and

248
00:15:02,542 --> 00:15:04,644
the browser will tell you when it arrives.

249
00:15:04,882 --> 00:15:08,092
On the right of the slide
are two ways to ask.

250
00:15:08,185 --> 00:15:12,920
You can do requestVideoFrameCallback,
which runs your code when a picture is

251
00:15:12,942 --> 00:15:18,047
actually shown, and it works on both the
Android or on both the Chrome and Safari.

252
00:15:18,403 --> 00:15:21,117
Or check the video readyState.

253
00:15:21,177 --> 00:15:24,551
When it gets to two, there
is a picture at that point.

254
00:15:25,052 --> 00:15:26,582
That is fix one.

255
00:15:26,792 --> 00:15:31,675
Wait for the event, not the
clock Fix two, the probe.

256
00:15:31,882 --> 00:15:33,572
Fix two goes one step further.

257
00:15:33,627 --> 00:15:36,598
Do not ask the camera
what size it is at all.

258
00:15:36,657 --> 00:15:38,703
Look at the picture and
measure it yourself.

259
00:15:38,906 --> 00:15:41,069
This is the code, and it is on GitHub.

260
00:15:41,137 --> 00:15:45,145
It takes one picture from the
video, shrinks it right down, and

261
00:15:45,157 --> 00:15:51,460
draws it in the top left corner
of a tiny square 16 pixels across.

262
00:15:51,674 --> 00:15:56,486
If the picture is landscape,
it fills a strip along the top.

263
00:15:57,382 --> 00:16:02,315
So the right corner has color,
and the bottom is left empty.

264
00:16:02,574 --> 00:16:07,133
If the picture is portrait, it
fills a strip down the left,

265
00:16:07,508 --> 00:16:09,862
so it is the other way round.

266
00:16:10,103 --> 00:16:12,843
Check those two corners
and you know the shape.

267
00:16:13,019 --> 00:16:15,786
Nothing to trust, just
what is really there.

268
00:16:15,920 --> 00:16:17,589
Now look at the first line.

269
00:16:17,747 --> 00:16:22,164
If there's no picture
yet, it returns nothing.

270
00:16:22,444 --> 00:16:23,779
It does not guess.

271
00:16:23,911 --> 00:16:25,665
That one line is this whole talk.

272
00:16:25,847 --> 00:16:29,349
Every problem in this story came from
something giving an answer before

273
00:16:29,351 --> 00:16:31,006
there was anything to answer about.

274
00:16:31,588 --> 00:16:35,084
In my app, the fix ended
up as three changes.

275
00:16:35,150 --> 00:16:39,409
One, I asked the camera for
landscape, its, its natural shape,

276
00:16:39,706 --> 00:16:41,559
and let the phone turn the picture.

277
00:16:41,889 --> 00:16:46,351
And that explains the strange night
phone earlier when I asked for landscape,

278
00:16:46,449 --> 00:16:48,114
landscape and got the portrait.

279
00:16:48,174 --> 00:16:52,560
The sensor is landscape, so ask in
its shape and the phone turns the

280
00:16:52,560 --> 00:16:54,418
picture the right way up for you.

281
00:16:54,664 --> 00:16:59,988
Two, I wait for the first picture
and measure it with this probe.

282
00:17:00,329 --> 00:17:05,733
Three, I record from a canvas, which
is a drawing surface in the page

283
00:17:06,040 --> 00:17:08,384
cut to the shape the probe saw.

284
00:17:08,572 --> 00:17:13,036
I had tried a canvas on night one,
but without knowing the real shape,

285
00:17:13,098 --> 00:17:17,904
all I got was a close-up of one eye
and a nose, and this time the probe

286
00:17:18,016 --> 00:17:20,605
told me what to cut, and it worked.

287
00:17:21,054 --> 00:17:23,887
So what test would have caught this?

288
00:17:23,929 --> 00:17:25,839
Not a more patient one.

289
00:17:25,848 --> 00:17:27,857
We already have four of those.

290
00:17:28,029 --> 00:17:29,301
You want the opposite.

291
00:17:29,601 --> 00:17:32,032
Read the value the moment it exists.

292
00:17:32,288 --> 00:17:36,442
Keep reading it on every frame
for the first second, and write

293
00:17:36,466 --> 00:17:38,523
down the time with each one.

294
00:17:38,678 --> 00:17:39,873
Then check one thing.

295
00:17:40,096 --> 00:17:41,270
Did it ever change?

296
00:17:41,377 --> 00:17:47,576
If it did, anything that read it early
got the wrong answer, and your log tells

297
00:17:47,576 --> 00:17:50,022
you how long the gap was on that phone.

298
00:17:50,387 --> 00:17:54,997
In this case, that, Test
fails on every first run.

299
00:17:55,226 --> 00:17:59,880
And here is the chaos engineering
version, which I like even more.

300
00:18:00,228 --> 00:18:03,826
If you think there is a gap,
make it bigger on purpose.

301
00:18:04,004 --> 00:18:08,533
In your test, fake the camera so
it gives you the wrong size for

302
00:18:08,541 --> 00:18:10,321
half a second before the right one.

303
00:18:10,555 --> 00:18:12,899
Then run your real code against it.

304
00:18:13,146 --> 00:18:18,079
If your code waits for the event, it
does not care how long the gap is.

305
00:18:18,187 --> 00:18:19,143
It passes.

306
00:18:19,399 --> 00:18:24,129
If it waits a fixed time or
reads straight away, it breaks.

307
00:18:25,163 --> 00:18:27,841
Once you know this pattern,
you start seeing it everywhere.

308
00:18:27,856 --> 00:18:31,338
Shaka Player is Google's video player,
and it is in a lot of streaming apps.

309
00:18:31,586 --> 00:18:35,264
A player like that downloads
videos in small pieces and

310
00:18:35,266 --> 00:18:36,470
hands them to the browser.

311
00:18:36,757 --> 00:18:40,185
When your connection changes,
it switches quality, throws away

312
00:18:40,187 --> 00:18:44,585
the pieces it had already, and
loads the new quality instead.

313
00:18:44,841 --> 00:18:48,790
Shaka had a bug open
which was number 10504.

314
00:18:48,878 --> 00:18:52,839
On Safari, after a quality switch,
the video jumped back a few

315
00:18:52,846 --> 00:18:54,414
seconds and played them again.

316
00:18:54,647 --> 00:18:58,309
The maintainer suspected Safari
itself, but nobody had measured it.

317
00:18:58,504 --> 00:19:00,205
So I did what I did with the camera.

318
00:19:00,254 --> 00:19:05,593
I ignored what the player said
and logged every picture the phone

319
00:19:05,704 --> 00:19:07,656
actually showed on my iPhone.

320
00:19:07,998 --> 00:19:10,670
Every switch jumped back 1.9

321
00:19:10,751 --> 00:19:11,866
and 7.1

322
00:19:11,879 --> 00:19:12,889
seconds in between.

323
00:19:13,179 --> 00:19:17,438
In Chrome on my PC, eight
switches out of eight were smooth.

324
00:19:17,713 --> 00:19:20,269
Then I did the most useful thing I know.

325
00:19:20,509 --> 00:19:24,619
I took the library out, a bare
page, just the browser, removed

326
00:19:24,851 --> 00:19:29,332
some videos, added new videos, same
jump, so the maintainer was right.

327
00:19:29,530 --> 00:19:34,178
The jump itself is in Safari's
engine, and that went to Apple on

328
00:19:34,178 --> 00:19:36,469
its own bug with the test page.

329
00:19:36,749 --> 00:19:38,403
That move is worth keeping.

330
00:19:38,755 --> 00:19:42,885
If the problem shows up with no library,
it is then definitely the platform.

331
00:19:43,128 --> 00:19:45,563
If it does not, then it's
definitely the library.

332
00:19:45,975 --> 00:19:50,507
If the library's own demo is fine,
then the problem is your code.

333
00:19:50,798 --> 00:19:54,230
But you still do not tell
people to wait for Apple because

334
00:19:54,294 --> 00:19:56,336
Apple may not decide to fix it.

335
00:19:56,564 --> 00:20:00,974
Shaka was asking for the new
video about two seconds too early.

336
00:20:01,501 --> 00:20:06,576
Change that on Apple browsers, give the
player a small nudge after the switch, and

337
00:20:06,576 --> 00:20:09,510
it was smooth eight out of eight times.

338
00:20:09,635 --> 00:20:17,774
It was just 18 lines, and it got merged
A third one I found is on hls.js,

339
00:20:17,901 --> 00:20:20,418
which, plays streaming
video in the browser.

340
00:20:20,502 --> 00:20:24,136
It had a bug open in October of 2024.

341
00:20:24,433 --> 00:20:29,220
In one mode, it starts using a piece of
video before the whole piece has arrived

342
00:20:29,277 --> 00:20:31,670
and reports how long each piece is.

343
00:20:31,952 --> 00:20:34,225
You will get things like 4.07

344
00:20:34,226 --> 00:20:36,363
seconds, two seconds, 2.07

345
00:20:36,363 --> 00:20:37,755
seconds, 3.93

346
00:20:37,757 --> 00:20:38,146
seconds.

347
00:20:38,195 --> 00:20:41,490
Each one of them was
actually really six seconds.

348
00:20:41,700 --> 00:20:44,273
It is the camera kind of issue again.

349
00:20:44,668 --> 00:20:48,106
A number read before the thing it
described had finished arriving.

350
00:20:48,122 --> 00:20:52,181
The fix takes the timing for
the later parts once they have

351
00:20:52,247 --> 00:20:54,419
arrived, and it was also merged.

352
00:20:54,581 --> 00:20:57,234
So that is three libraries,
two browsers and one habit.

353
00:20:57,475 --> 00:21:01,118
Reading a value before the thing
behind it has actually finished.

354
00:21:01,510 --> 00:21:05,260
Look for that in your own systems
and ask, "Is this actually

355
00:21:05,261 --> 00:21:07,919
ready or has it just started?"

356
00:21:09,556 --> 00:21:12,168
Let me bring all of this
right back down to the chaos

357
00:21:12,170 --> 00:21:13,477
engineering we're talking about.

358
00:21:13,574 --> 00:21:16,031
Everything in this talk is
the loop you already know.

359
00:21:16,295 --> 00:21:19,448
Say what normal looks like,
portrait in, portrait out.

360
00:21:19,687 --> 00:21:21,712
Make a guess about what breaks it.

361
00:21:21,956 --> 00:21:25,074
The size is read before
the first picture exists.

362
00:21:25,926 --> 00:21:30,547
Then run an experiment that could prove
you wrong and measure what happens.

363
00:21:30,593 --> 00:21:32,737
The timer was the experiment.

364
00:21:33,052 --> 00:21:39,353
The stretch window on slide 14 is the
same experiment made bigger on purpose.

365
00:21:39,717 --> 00:21:42,818
With a bug in someone else's
code, there is one more step.

366
00:21:42,844 --> 00:21:47,681
You cannot run the fix yourself, so the
report is the experiment you hand over.

367
00:21:47,972 --> 00:21:53,280
The bottom of the slide is what went
into each and every one of this one,

368
00:21:53,312 --> 00:21:55,871
and is what went into mine also.

369
00:21:56,139 --> 00:22:00,730
The exact device and version so
they can run it on the same thing.

370
00:22:00,982 --> 00:22:04,532
What I asked for and what I
got as numbers, so it is a

371
00:22:04,543 --> 00:22:06,456
measurement and not an opinion.

372
00:22:06,755 --> 00:22:11,002
A test page with nothing else
on it, so the only thing being

373
00:22:11,004 --> 00:22:13,106
tested is the thing in question.

374
00:22:13,315 --> 00:22:17,062
Steps that show it in five
seconds so anyone can repeat

375
00:22:17,071 --> 00:22:21,747
it, and what I had already ruled
out so nobody spends time there.

376
00:22:22,024 --> 00:22:25,389
Notice what is not on that
list, a suggested fix.

377
00:22:25,455 --> 00:22:26,964
I do not know their code.

378
00:22:27,024 --> 00:22:28,244
I don't know your code.

379
00:22:28,502 --> 00:22:30,644
What I do know is my measurement.

380
00:22:30,876 --> 00:22:33,912
When a report reads like that,
the other side repeats the

381
00:22:33,914 --> 00:22:36,161
experiment instead of debating it.

382
00:22:36,330 --> 00:22:40,658
That is why the times at the top of
the slides are short, not because

383
00:22:40,863 --> 00:22:43,083
the code that I shared was clever.

384
00:22:43,332 --> 00:22:48,178
it was because I gave them what
they needed to be able to, you

385
00:22:48,178 --> 00:22:52,134
know, replicate the chaos and be
able to actually solve the problem.

386
00:22:52,361 --> 00:22:57,220
So when you hit a flaky failure in a
system that you do not own, do not send

387
00:22:57,220 --> 00:23:04,526
a story, send an experiment So here
are four things you can use on Monday.

388
00:23:04,762 --> 00:23:08,413
They are on the slide, so grab
a screenshot if you need one.

389
00:23:08,413 --> 00:23:13,622
One, a value that is eventually
right passes every test that waits.

390
00:23:13,634 --> 00:23:16,644
So test by reading early.

391
00:23:17,010 --> 00:23:20,036
Two, wait for the event, not the clock.

392
00:23:20,265 --> 00:23:26,068
A sleep that fixes a timing bug
is a race you have not lost yet.

393
00:23:26,551 --> 00:23:31,049
Three, when the report and the real
thing disagree, believe the real thing.

394
00:23:31,329 --> 00:23:34,581
The camera said landscape,
the picture said portrait.

395
00:23:34,697 --> 00:23:36,487
Believe the picture.

396
00:23:37,299 --> 00:23:41,776
Four, measure the gap before
you argue about the delay.

397
00:23:42,099 --> 00:23:46,703
Read early, read often, and stretch
it on purpose to see what breaks.

398
00:23:47,178 --> 00:23:51,917
And a bonus, if you debug with
an AI agent like I do, give it

399
00:23:52,083 --> 00:23:53,874
the numbers, not a description.

400
00:23:53,884 --> 00:23:58,040
Nineteen-twenty by ten-eighty at
ten fifty-nine milliseconds.

401
00:23:58,178 --> 00:24:01,797
Ten-eighty by nineteen-twenty at
fourteen seventy-three milliseconds.

402
00:24:01,836 --> 00:24:04,284
It's a very hard thing to argue with.

403
00:24:04,360 --> 00:24:08,373
It-- If you say things like, "It comes
out sideways," it's easy to argue

404
00:24:08,374 --> 00:24:10,671
with that and it will argue with you.

405
00:24:11,556 --> 00:24:14,737
So all I can just say is thank you.

406
00:24:14,990 --> 00:24:20,172
When four green ticks disagree with what
you see on the screen, check the ticks.

407
00:24:20,416 --> 00:24:21,313
that is my talk.

408
00:24:21,333 --> 00:24:27,310
There is no live Q&A on the recording, so
if you have questions or you run the probe

409
00:24:27,341 --> 00:24:31,868
on your own app and you see something
odd, find me on LinkedIn, Fuad Laguda.

410
00:24:32,087 --> 00:24:36,018
My messages are open and I would
really like to hear what you find.

411
00:24:36,250 --> 00:24:40,964
the code is on GitHub,
laguda-fuad-tosin/web-teleprompter.

412
00:24:41,190 --> 00:24:42,580
It's MIT license.

413
00:24:42,721 --> 00:24:47,291
The teleprompter is part of, the app
that I run, and I really look forward

414
00:24:47,291 --> 00:24:50,326
to hearing what you would d-do with it.

415
00:24:50,665 --> 00:24:53,706
Anyway, thank you for watching
and enjoy the rest of Conf42

416
00:24:53,714 --> 00:24:55,187
Chaos Engineering 2026.

417
00:24:55,231 --> 00:24:57,036
You have been wonderful.

418
00:24:57,127 --> 00:24:57,822
Thanks for listening

